{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Kats2016-aSi-Ag-aSi-Ag-broadband",
  "Source": "M. A. Kats and F. Capasso, \"Optical absorbers based on strong interference in ultra-thin films,\" Laser Photonics Rev., 1-15 (2016) (Early View; volume and final pages not printed in this copy). doi:10.1002/lpor.201600098 - Fig. 5(a), p. 8 (from ref. [61], Lee et al. APL 108, 031107 (2016)): a-Si (12 nm) / Ag (30 nm) / a-Si (24 nm) / Ag (80 nm) / Glass, light from the top",
  "Notes": "Broadband visible absorber made of stacked ultra-thin Ag/a-Si cavities (with top a-Si layer)\nSpecification: Broadband absorption in the visible (simulated).\nReported: Fig. 5(b) red solid/dotted curves (read off the plot, approximate): total absorption ~95 % peak near 480-500 nm, ~90 % around 550 nm, above ~80 % from ~450 to ~590 nm, falling to ~10 % by 700 nm and ~5 % at 800 nm; absorption in the a-Si (right axis) slightly lower.\nNotes: Listed top (light side) to bottom as in the figure. Glass index not given; n = 1.52 assumed (irrelevant in practice, the 80 nm Ag is nearly opaque). a-Si and Ag optical constants not given in the review (library data substituted). Normal incidence assumed (arrow in Fig. 5(a)). Total metal+semiconductor thickness 146 nm.\nLayers from Fig. 5(a), p. 8 (from ref. [61], Lee et al. APL 108, 031107 (2016)): a-Si (12 nm) / Ag (30 nm) / a-Si (24 nm) / Ag (80 nm) / Glass, light from the top; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours A peak 97.0 % at 580 nm, > 80 % from 440 to 620 nm; paper A ~95 % peak near 480-500 nm, > 80 % from ~450 to ~590 nm (read off Fig. 5b). The paper does not give its a-Si and Ag data; Pierce & Spicer a-Si and Johnson & Christy Ag are used here.",
  "Wavelength": [
